      "inputTokens": 103,
      "outputTokens": 728,
      "costUsd": 0.0066035,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's a phone's storage on an ordinary ruler, from zero to two hundred and fifty six gigabytes. A text message is about a kilobyte, a photo about three megabytes, and a film about four gigabytes. But on this ruler, the text and the photo are squashed almost onto zero, and the film sits near the left. Only the whole phone has room. When does an ordinary axis fail like this, and what kind of scale gives every file its own place?"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-t04/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 846,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 0.7810000000000024,
        "after": "before.",
        "before": "One"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "text": "magnitude",
          "start": 15.329999999999995,
          "end": 15.779999999999994
        },
        {
          "text": "zero",
          "start": 27.477000000000075,
          "end": 27.749800000000075
        }
      ],
      "seconds": 28.198,
      "peakFraction": 0.882904052734375,
      "fullScaleSamples": 0,
      "silencesAtLeast1_5s": [],
      "lowScoreWords": [
        {
          "word": "a",
          "start": 11.120999999999992,
          "end": 11.160999999999992,
          "score": 0.499
        },
        {
          "word": "a",
          "start": 25.236000000000075,
          "end": 25.296000000000074,
          "score": 0.333
        }
      ],
      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 0,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 314,
      "outputTokens": 2188,
      "costUsd": 0.019849000000000002,
      "pronunciationText": "The trouble is the range. A kilobyte is a thousand bytes, a megabyte is a million, a gigabyte a billion, and a terabyte a million million. Each name is a thousand times the one before. One factor of ten is called an order of magnitude, so a text message and a full phone are more than eight orders of magnitude apart. On an ordinary axis, the biggest value sets the scale, so everything a thousand times smaller is crushed against zero."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-03",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-t04/scene-03.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 957,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 0.6220000000000638,
        "after": "number.",
        "before": "Equal"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "text": "value",
          "start": 1.123,
          "end": 1.4238333333333333
        },
        {
          "text": "ten",
          "start": 2.467,
