      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 273,
      "outputTokens": 1952,
      "costUsd": 0.0177045,
      "pronunciationText": "So when should you use one? Compare two sets of data. The brightness of some stars runs from about three times ten to the minus thirteen, to one point four times ten to the minus eight: a factor of about forty seven thousand. On a log scale, they spread out nicely. But the heights of a class run from one fifty two to one eighty seven centimetres: a factor of only one point two three. A log scale adds nothing for such a narrow range, and heights are compared by differences anyway. So use a log scale when the values span several powers of ten, and when comparing by factors makes sense."
    },
    {
