      "successfulRequestCount": 0,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 0,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 0,
      "outputTokens": 0,
      "costUsd": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Decibels are a log scale too. The sound level L is ten times log base ten of I over I nought. I is the intensity, the power of the sound arriving on each square metre, and I nought is ten to the minus twelve, a conventional reference near the threshold of hearing. Multiply the intensity by ten, and the log goes up by one, so the level goes up by ten decibels. So a factory measuring ninety five decibels, against a limit of eighty five, has a reading about twelve percent higher, but the sound isn't twelve percent stronger. Ten decibels more means ten times the intensity. And a three decibel rise is a factor of ten to the point three, about two."
    },
