      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 115,
      "outputTokens": 915,
      "costUsd": 0.0082925,
      "pronunciationText": "Earthquake magnitude can work the same way, with no ten in front. In a simplified local magnitude, M is log base ten of A over A nought, where ay is the size of the wiggle on a seismograph, measured at the same distance with the same instrument, so each step of one means ten times the amplitude. Earthquake ay is magnitude five. Earthquake B has a seismograph trace fifty times as tall. Its magnitude is five plus log of fifty, about six point seven. Fifty times the wiggle shows up as only one point seven more on the scale."
    },
